HISTSH @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-histsh, @ARCH@) - EARLY TEST RELEASE
=====================================================================

HISTSH gives DCL a command history that lasts across sessions, the way bash
keeps ~/.bash_history: log in, press up-arrow, and your commands from earlier
sessions are there. Everything else is plain DCL, unchanged.

How it works
------------
DCL's RECALL /OUTPUT and /INPUT only work when typed at the $ prompt; inside
a command procedure they do nothing. So HISTSH runs your DCL session on a
pseudo-terminal and passes every keystroke through: DCL's line editing,
Ctrl/Y and Ctrl/T behave as always. After each command it quietly asks DCL
what was run (with RECALL commands you never see) and appends it at once to
your history file, which all your sessions share.

At the $ prompt:
  up-arrow, Ctrl/B   the previous command from your history (all sessions,
                     not just this one); edit it with DCL's usual keys
  down-arrow         the next one; past the newest, an empty line
  Ctrl/R             search the history, as in bash: type to narrow it,
                     Ctrl/R again for an older match, Delete to shorten,
                     Return to run the match, Escape or an arrow key to put
                     it on the line, Ctrl/G to cancel

Elsewhere (a continuation line, a program such as EDT, typing ahead) keys go
to DCL or the program as usual.

History lives in SYS$LOGIN:HISTSH_HISTORY.LIS (owner access only), or in the
file the logical name HISTSH$HISTORY names. It keeps the newest HISTSH$SIZE
commands (default 2000) for up-arrow; the file is cut back when it grows past
twice that. Other sessions' commands show up in a session at its next login.
Commands typed while your LOGIN.COM is still running are kept.

Setting it up
-------------
1. At boot, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:
       $ @SYS$STARTUP:HISTSH$STARTUP.COM
   (PRODUCT INSTALL has already run it once, so HISTSH$ROOT and HISTSH exist.)

2. Add as the LAST line of SYS$MANAGER:SYLOGIN.COM, so the session HISTSH runs
   inherits everything SYLOGIN sets up (symbols, logical names, keys, prompt):
       $ IF F$SEARCH("HISTSH:HISTSH_START.COM") .NES. "" THEN @HISTSH:HISTSH_START
   It returns at once for batch, network and detached processes, for logins
   not on a terminal, and for users without the identifier below.

3. Switch users on with AUTHORIZE:
       UAF> ADD/IDENTIFIER HISTSH$USER              ! once
       UAF> GRANT/IDENTIFIER HISTSH$USER username
   It takes effect at the user's next login. REVOKE/IDENTIFIER switches a user
   off again. (The logical HISTSH$IDENTIFIER names a different identifier.)

To try it without any of this: with HISTSH defined, a user can type
       $ HISTSH_PROMPT = F$ENVIRONMENT("PROMPT")
       $ RUN HISTSH:HISTSH
and LOGOUT from that session returns to the first.

Limits of this release
----------------------
- A SET PROMPT typed at the $ prompt is handled (HISTSH puts back the marker
  it finds DCL's prompt by); a prompt changed inside a command procedure is
  not, and saving stops for that session.
- The user's LOGIN.COM is taken to be SYS$LOGIN:LOGIN.COM (UAF /LGICMD is
  not read).
- Each session uses two processes: the user's DCL is a subprocess, so it counts
  against PRCLM and shares the job's quotas, and logging out shows two
  "logged out" lines.
- As with bash, anything typed on a command line (a password given as a
  parameter, for example) is recorded. Passwords typed at a prompt are not.
- At the $ prompt Ctrl/R is HISTSH's search, not DCL's "redisplay line".

Removing
--------
Take the SYLOGIN line out, then PRODUCT REMOVE HISTSH. It deassigns HISTSH$ROOT
and HISTSH. The identifier and users' history files are kept.

Source, issues and the rest of the family: github.com/issinoho/vms-histsh and
openvms.issinoho.com. MIT licence (LICENSE. in the installation directory).
